# Anti-slop conventions

Encodes the most common AI-generated code-quality failures.

## Banned filename suffixes

Do not commit files matching `*_v2.*`, `*_new.*`, `*_old.*`,
`*_backup.*`, `*_fixed.*`, `*_temp.*`, `*_wip.*`. These are AI
debugging residue. If a file is canonical, give it a proper name.

## No `console.log` in production paths

Use a structured logger when one exists in the project. Otherwise
leave a `// TODO(P1): wire up logger` and route to `console.error`
for the interim. Do not commit ad-hoc `console.log` calls in `lib/`,
`app/`, or any production-reachable path.

## Grep before creating shared utilities

Before implementing a new utility (date formatter, error handler,
HTTP client, retry wrapper), confirm one does not already exist:

```bash
grep -rn "formatDate\|handleError\|withRetry" src/ lib/ app/ | head -20
```

If a canonical version exists, import it. Do not create a parallel
implementation.

## `!` non-null assertion requires a comment

`!` bypasses null-checking and hides runtime bugs. Allowed only with
an adjacent one-line comment explaining why it is safe:

```ts
// safe: validated by Zod schema above
const id = parsed.data.id!;
```
